+## `jj git ref push`
+
+Push one revision to a raw Git ref on a remote
+
+This low-level command is intended for application-specific refs such as Gerrit's `refs/for/main`. It does not create or track a bookmark.
+
+Each invocation must choose whether to push unconditionally, require the ref to be absent, or require it to point to an expected object ID.
+
+Unlike `jj git push`, this command does not check for private commits, missing descriptions or identities, or conflicts. It also does not use `git.sign-on-push`; it pushes the selected Git commit as stored.
+
+**Usage:** `jj git ref push [OPTIONS] <--force|--expected-at <OBJECT_ID>|--expected-absent> <REVISION> <REF>`
+
+###### **Arguments:**
+
+* `<REVISION>` — The revision to push
+* `<REF>` — The fully qualified destination ref, such as `refs/for/main`
+
+###### **Options:**
+
+* `--remote <REMOTE>` — The remote to push to (only named remotes are supported)
+
+   This defaults to the `git.push` setting. If that is not configured, the only remote or the remote named "origin" is used.
+* `--force` — Push without checking the current position of the remote ref
+* `--expected-at <OBJECT_ID>` — Push only if the remote ref is currently at this full Git object ID
+* `--expected-absent` — Push only if the remote ref does not exist
+* `-o`, `--option <OPTION>` — Git push options (can be repeated)
+
+
+
 ## `jj git remote`
 
 Manage Git remotes
